CRYPTO(1)                 USER COMMANDS                 CRYPTO(1)NAME     crypto - a program to generate and/or solve cryptogramsSYNOPSIS     crypto [-cegrsu] [file(s)]DESCRIPTION     _C_r_y_p_t_o is a fun program for playing around with cryptograms.     It  can  be used to encode text with a random or user-picked     cypher, to interactively solve cryptogram puzzles,  to  non-     interactively  apply  a solution to a text file, and to save     the results to the file of your choice.     In normal usage, text is taken from the specified  file  (or     the  standard  input), encoded (if needed) and displayed for     you to solve. You can save the current puzzle at  any  time,     and  take  up where you left off later on.  If the cypher is     picked by (or supplied to) _c_r_y_p_t_o, it will inform  you  when     you have correctly solved the puzzle.  You will also be able     to cheat and see the correct solution one letter at a  time.     A simple, yet entertaining example is the command:     fortune -l | crypto -r     which encrypts a long fortune with a simple roational cypher     and lets you solve it.  For a more difficult time, try:     fortune -s | crypto -e     _C_r_y_p_t_o can also be  used  in  a  non-interactive  manner  by     redirecting the output to a file.  In this case, the text is     read in, encoded if -e, -r, or -c  was  specified,  and  the     resulting _p_u_z_z_l_e is output.  If, however, a guess was speci-     fied with the -g option, the guess is applied to the  puzzle     and the resulting _s_o_l_u_t_i_o_n is output.  When redirecting, you     will probably want to use the -n option to avoid  outputting     the brace-commands (see below).  For example:     crypto -n -gzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba puzz >solved     _C_r_y_p_t_o uses a simple, text-based output format to  save  the     puzzle,  guess, cypher, and title in a file.  These take the     form of "brace-commands", each  on  a  line  of  their  own,     interspersed  into the puzzle text.  {-gabc...xyz} specifies     the current guess, {-cabc...xyz} specifes  the  cypher,  and     {-tTitle}  specifies  the title.  Any or all of these may be     ommitted to take on their default values.  In addition,  the     command {-end} allows you to separate the file into multiple     puzzles, just as if you'd specified multiple file  names  on     the  command-line.   Each puzzle has a unique guess, cypher,     and title.Sun Release 3.2        Last change: LOCAL                       1CRYPTO(1)                 USER COMMANDS                 CRYPTO(1)     When multiple puzzles are loaded, you will be presented with     a  menu of choices for your initial puzzle.  From there, the     save command (^S) saves all the puzzles into a single  file.     This  menu  can be visited at any time with the menu command     (^M).     Here is a complete list of the interactive  commands  avail-     able.   You  can get a summary of this list with the command     _c_r_y_p_t_o -_i or by typing '?' while running _c_r_y_p_t_o.     <let1><let2> guess the substitution cypher  a  letter  at  a                  time.  This is your primary tool in solving the                  cryptogram.  Example:  xb     <let><space> undefine <let>'s current guess.  <space> can be                  any  non-letter.   This  lets  you  selectively                  remove your letters and try again.     ^U<let>      the  alternate  way  to  Undefine  a   letter's                  current guess.     ^X           Undefine ALL the  guessed  letters.   Good  for                  starting afresh.     ^C           Cheat -- if I know the solution, I will correct                  one  letter  in the puzzle each time you decide                  to cheat.     ^S<file>     Save the  current  puzzle,  guess,  and  cypher                  information in a file.  This allows you to con-                  tinue where ever you left off with the  command                  crypto <file>     ^D           Define the current guess to  be  the  solution.                  Useful  before  a  save to define the cypher to                  save along with  the  puzzle  (if  not  already                  known).     ^H           Hint -- select a word to perform  a  dictionary                  search  on.  This is useful for solving unknown                  cyphers.  All  the  currently  guessed  letters                  affect  the  matching  of dictionary words into                  the cypher word.  While in hint-mode, type  '?'                  for a brief command summary.     ^N           go to the Next puzzle in a multi-puzzle file.     ^P           go to the Previous  puzzle  in  a  multi-puzzle                  file.     ^M           presents a Menu of the current  puzzles  avail-                  able for selection.Sun Release 3.2        Last change: LOCAL                       2CRYPTO(1)                 USER COMMANDS                 CRYPTO(1)     ^A           toggle the Alpha display between the  two  dif-                  ferent output modes.     ^T           enter a (new) Title for this puzzle.     ^Q, <Esc>    Quit -- leave the program.  Confirms  the  exit                  request  if  we  believe you might like to save                  the puzzle(s) first.     ^L, ^R       Redraw the screen.     6, 4         Move a wide cryptogram left/right by 4 chars.     2, 8         Move a long cryptogram up/down by 2 lines.     7, 1         Move to the far left/right of a wide puzzle.     9, 3         Move to the top/bottom of a long puzzle.     The following are valid command-line options:     -u      Force all input to Upper-case.     -e      Encode the input stream with a random cypher.     -r      encode using a  simpler,  rot-based  random  cypher.             You  can  specify a specific rotation by including a             number from 1 to 26 after the r, as in:  -r13.     -cSTR   encode using the Cypher STR for encryption.  STR  is             the 26 letters of the alphabet in whatever order you             choose.     -gSTR   initialize the Guessed letters to STR.     -n      indicates that No brace-commands are to be  included             in the save or pipe output.  Brace-commands are nor-             mally output to indicate the current cypher,  guess,             and/or title.     FILE    read input from one or more FILEs, instead of stdin.     -i      output a summary of the Interactive commands.     -h      output a summary of the command-line options.     -       the null option can be used to avoid an  empty  com-             mand  tail,  which would output a short command-line             summary.FILES     /usr/dict/wordsSun Release 3.2        Last change: LOCAL                       3CRYPTO(1)                 USER COMMANDS                 CRYPTO(1)AUTHOR     _C_r_y_p_t_o was written by Wayne Davison.  Special  thanx  go  to     Bruce  Holloway, whose "figure" program provided inspiration     into curses & the dictionary lookup.Sun Release 3.2        Last change: LOCAL                       4